Q: ul ref not getting updated when a list element is removed and as such the focus goes back to the body A: <p>This is a React render timing issue. When <code>focusElementOnClose</code> runs, the state update that removes the object has been <em>scheduled</em> but React hasn't re-rendered yet, so the deleted card's button is still in the DOM. That's why your <code>ul</code> ref still shows the removed element — and why <code>setTimeout</code> works, since it defers until after the paint.</p>
<p>The idiomatic React solution is to store the intended focus target and act on it in a <code>useEffect</code>, which runs <em>after</em> the DOM has been updated to reflect the new state.</p>
<pre><code>const [focusTargetId, setFocusTargetId] = useState&lt;string | null&gt;(null);

useEffect(() =&gt; {
  if (!focusTargetId) return;

  const el = document.getElementById(focusTargetId) as HTMLButtonElement | null;
  el?.focus();
  setFocusTargetId(null);
}, [focusTargetId]);
</code></pre>
<p>Then rewrite <code>focusElementOnClose</code> to only <em>compute and store</em> the target ID rather than imperatively focusing:</p>
<pre><code>const focusElementOnClose = (
  object?: Object | null,
  wasRemoved: boolean = false
) =&gt; {
  if (wasRemoved) {
    const objectCardButtonList = document.querySelectorAll(
      `button[id^=&quot;adl_objectCard_optionsButton_&quot;]`
    ) as NodeListOf&lt;HTMLButtonElement&gt;;
    const objectCardButtonsArray = Array.from(objectCardButtonList);

    const indexOfRemoved = objectCardButtonsArray.findIndex(
      (button) =&gt; button.id === `adl_objectCard_optionsButton_${object?.id}`
    );

    // The next sibling in the current DOM (before re-render) will become
    // the element at the same index after the removed one disappears.
    // So target index + 1 if it exists, otherwise fall back to add button.
    const nextButton = objectCardButtonsArray[indexOfRemoved + 1];

    if (nextButton) {
      setFocusTargetId(nextButton.id);
    } else if (indexOfRemoved &gt; 0) {
      // Nothing after it — try the previous one
      setFocusTargetId(objectCardButtonsArray[indexOfRemoved - 1].id);
    } else {
      // List will be empty
      setFocusTargetId('adl_addObjectButton');
    }
  } else {
    // Cancel — focus the same button (DOM won't change, safe to do immediately)
    const objectCardButton = document.querySelector(
      `button[id=&quot;adl_objectCard_optionsButton_${object?.id}&quot;]`
    ) as HTMLButtonElement | null;
    objectCardButton?.focus();
  }
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> <code>setFocusTargetId</code> triggers a re-render. By the time the <code>useEffect</code> fires, React has committed the updated DOM (without the deleted card), so <code>getElementById</code> finds the correct next button and focuses it cleanly.</p>
<p>A couple of things worth noting on the logic side too — your original code had <code>objectCardButtonsArray[0].focus()</code> unconditionally at the bottom of the <code>if (nextButton)</code> block, which would always override the <code>nextButton.focus()</code> call above it. The rewrite above also adds a &quot;previous button&quot; fallback for when the deleted item was the last in the list, which is a better UX than jumping to the add button in that case.</p>

Q: Why can&#39;t I change the rotation of sprites? A: <p>You're expecting to rotate by degrees, but <code>rotate</code> uses the default <a href="https://p5js.org/reference/p5/angleMode/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">angleMode</a> with radians:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Functions such as <code>rotate()</code> and <code>sin()</code> expect angles measured <strong>radians by default</strong>.</p>
<p>Calling <code>angleMode(DEGREES)</code> switches to degrees.<br />
Calling <code>angleMode(RADIANS)</code> switches back to radians.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Change to degrees to rotate by x degrees:</p>
<pre><code>angleMode(DEGREES);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function setup() {

  angleMode(DEGREES);

  createCanvas(350, 350);
  sprite = new Sprite(200, 200, 100, 100);
  sprite.rotation = 45;
}

function update() {
  clear();
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@1.11.4/lib/p5.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@1.11.4/lib/addons/p5.sound.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://p5play.org/v3/planck.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://p5play.org/v3/p5play.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to set the precision of JsNumber output in spray.json? A: <p>You can define a custom <code>JsonWriter</code> to do this (within your protocol or wherever else with a higher-precedence implicit scope):</p>
<pre><code>implicit object RoundedDoubleJsonWriter extends JsonWriter[Double] {
  def write(d: Double) =
    JsNumber(BigDecimal(d).setScale(4, BigDecimal.RoundingMode.HALF_UP))
}
</code></pre>
<p>Change the rounding mode/method to taste.</p>

Q: How can I dump all a Go process&#39;s stacks without killing it? A: <p>You can set up a handler like that using code something like this:</p>
<pre><code>import (
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;os&quot;
    &quot;os/signal&quot;
    &quot;runtime&quot;
    &quot;syscall&quot;
)

func main() {
    sigChan := make(chan os.Signal, 1)
    go func() {
        stacktrace := make([]byte, 8192)
        for _ = range sigChan {
            length := runtime.Stack(stacktrace, true)
            fmt.Println(string(stacktrace[:length]))
        }
    }()
    signal.Notify(sigChan, syscall.SIGQUIT)


    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>SIGQUIT</code> signal will now be caught and sent to the given channel.  The <code>runtime.Stack</code> function is then used to format the stack trace into a prepared buffer (if it is larger than the buffer, it will be truncated), and then printed.</p>
